\chapter{Comparison with Contemporary Systems}
\label{ch:comparison}

Telix occupies a specific point in a design space already populated by
mature systems.  This chapter locates Telix relative to them, and states
plainly what is borrowed, what is new, and why the new parts are not
already provided by a configuration of an existing system.

\section{The Design Space}
\label{sec:design_space}

Two axes organise the space.  The first is the \emph{isolation
mechanism}: hardware MMU boundaries (classic microkernels) versus
language-enforced safety (safe-language kernels).  The second is the
\emph{memory-management stance}: conventional per-page descriptor
arrays versus extent-based (morsel) accounting.

