\section{Why Not Build on seL4?}
\label{sec:vs_sel4}

seL4 \footcite{klei09} is the reference point: a microkernel whose
correctness is machine-checked end to end.  If the goal were ``a
verified kernel,'' seL4 already exists and has done for over a decade.

Telix differs in three ways that are not incremental:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Memory model.} seL4 verifies a kernel whose page
    allocation is conventional; it does not provide page clustering,
    morsel accounting, or the sublinear-metadata goal, which are
    properties of the \emph{memory manager}, not the kernel's
    correctness.  Telix's memory design is orthogonal to, and could in
    principle be layered atop, seL4's verified core --- but seL4 does
    not provide it today.

  \item \textbf{Verification style.} seL4's proof is a heroic,
    single-implementation, per-ISA effort.  Telix's stated approach is
    compositional: hardware proofs (Tessera) independent of the kernel,
    then a machine-interface Iris layer, then manual Iris proofs.  Whether this
    is \emph{easier} than the seL4 path is unproven; the claim is only
    that it is a different decomposition.

  \item \textbf{Scope.} seL4 is a kernel, not an OS; its userspace story
    (pagers, personalities, filesystems) is deliberately out of scope.
    Telix is a design \emph{for the whole system}, including the
    external pager and personality layers that seL4 leaves to its users.
\end{enumerate}

The honest comparison: Telix does not compete with seL4 on ``verified
kernel'' --- seL4 wins there and is real.  Telix competes on the
\emph{memory-management and scheduling} design layered above the
kernel boundary, where seL4 is silent.

\section{Why Not Build on Zircon?}
\label{sec:vs_zircon}

Zircon \footcite{fuchsia_zircon} is the production-proven microkernel
with the capability model (handles and channels) closest to Telix's.
The structural isomorphism is close enough that the personality chapter
(\S\ref{sec:nextgen_abis}) claims Zircon syscalls translate ``near 1:1''
to Telix capabilities.

Where Telix diverges:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Zircon retains conventional per-page memory accounting and a
    conventional kernel thread model.  Telix's morsel allocator,
    external pagers, and M:N stackless futures are not present in
    Zircon, and are not straightforwardly addable without reworking its
    core.

  \item Zircon is tightly coupled to Fuchsia's component framework
    (FIDL, components, packages).  Telix's personality model is
    deliberately ABI-agnostic (Linux, Windows, Zircon, OpenHarmony),
    treating Zircon as \emph{one} personality among several rather than
    the native substrate.
\end{itemize}

\section{Why Not Build on Redox or Theseus?}
\label{sec:vs_rust}

Redox \footcite{redox} and Theseus \footcite{theseus20} are the two
prominent Rust kernels, and the natural first question is ``why is this
not an incremental change to one of them?''

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Redox}] is a microkernel with a POSIX personality,
    built for memory safety in Rust.  It is the closest existing
    implementation to Telix's \emph{personality} ambition.  It does not
    provide page clustering, morsel accounting, external pagers, M:N
    stackless futures, or the cluster/SSI model.  Telix's contributions
    are at the memory-management and scheduling layer, not the
    ``write a kernel in Rust'' layer, so Redox is a compatibility
    \emph{peer} rather than a superset.

  \item[\textbf{Theseus}] explores language-level state management
    (cell-based composition, live evolution) rather than memory
    granularity.  Its isolation is Rust-based throughout; it does not
    attempt the hybrid MMU/language boundary Telix proposes, and its
    memory model is conventional.  Theseus's contribution --- that safe
    languages enable OS restructuring --- is a premise Telix shares but
    applies to a different subsystem.
\end{description}

RedLeaf \footcite{redleaf20} is the most instructive neighbour: it
demonstrated that Rust's type system can isolate device drivers inside
one address space without per-driver MMU domains.  That result is the
closest published evidence for Telix's aspirational framekernel
boundary (\S\ref{sec:aspirational}) --- and it also marks the boundary
of what is known: RedLeaf isolated \emph{drivers}, not the full set of
mutually-distrusting servers Telix proposes to co-locate.

\section{The Asterinas Relationship}
\label{sec:vs_asterinas}

Asterinas \footcite{peng24} made the framekernel idea concrete: a whole
OS in one Rust-safe address space, with no MMU isolation between
servers, and no performance-costly context switches.  Telix borrows the
framekernel technique but stops far short of Asterinas' maximalism:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Asterinas puts \emph{everything} in one address space; Telix
    pulls in only thin, performance-critical layers and leaves the rest
    as isolated processes.

  \item Asterinas' isolation argument is uniform (Rust everywhere);
    Telix's is heterogeneous (Rust inside, MMU outside), which is both
    the source of its flexibility and its least-proven aspect
    (\S\ref{sec:aspirational}).
\end{itemize}

The relationship is best described as: Telix is a multikernel
(\Barrelfish) of \emph{partial} framekernels (\Asterinas), where the
framekernel is an optimisation layer rather than the whole TCB.

\section{What Is Actually New}
\label{sec:what_is_new}

Stripping away the borrowed parts leaves a short, defensible list of
novel claims:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Morsel/extent-based coremapless memory} as the
    \emph{primary} accounting unit (borrowed from main-memory databases
    \footcite{kemp14}, applied kernel-wide), with the Z-granularity
    no-external-fragmentation guarantee (\S\ref{sec:mem_clustering}).

  \item \textbf{The Z-attractor distributional argument} for TLB reach
    --- that clustering's main effect is to raise the \emph{mean}
    translation size by herding the left tail, not to produce a few
    large superpages (\S\ref{sec:mem_clustering}).

  \item \textbf{The hybrid isolation boundary} --- MMU outside the
    framekernel, Rust inside, with an explicit degradation path --- as
    a distinct (if unproven) point between Asterinas and seL4
    (\S\ref{sec:aspirational}).

  \item \textbf{The verification decomposition} --- hardware proofs
    independent of the kernel, bridged by a machine-interface Iris
    layer, then manual Iris proofs --- as an alternative to the seL4
    monolithic-proof path (\S\ref{sec:unified_stack}).

  \item \textbf{M:N stackless-future execution with stack-carrier
    preemption} as a revival of scheduler activations, contingent on a
    serialisation mechanism for migratable futures (\S\ref{sec:preemption}).
\end{enumerate}

Every one of these is a claim to be tested; none is yet established by
measurement.  The value of this chapter is to make the list explicit,
so that the claims can be evaluated on their merits rather than buried
in the borrowed scaffolding.
